A||||2||Gokart||Code 32 problem solved!||||14||
Z||000000||Gokart||03-10-2000||08:13 PM||||Didn't want to jinx myself like last week when I put in the EGR solenoid in my GT from my parts car, but 3 days now and no more code! Thanks mwbackus for the map to take apart the EGR solenoid. I cleaned it out with electrical parts cleaner. It wasn't that dirty, but I'm sure it helped. I believe the problem was with the plug that goes into the solenoid. One of the prongs was split in two. I had one mechanic tell me that as long as I had metal to metal the connection was good, but after showing it to my UPS mechanic at work he said that it could make a difference. So I cut the wires behind the plug and put the one in from my parts car and now runs fine. So I'm not sure if it was the cleaning that did it or the plug replacement, but I'm just thankful my engine lights not coming on anymore! ||||reg||14
Z||000001||mshill||03-12-2000||11:00 PM||||Before I tore my engine apart I was getting a code 32 when traveling at 70 mph for 5-10 minutes.  I was going to put the plenum back on tonight when I inspected all of the vacuum lines and found one of the two that goes to the EGR soleniod was cracked about half way through.  I would bet that when I get it back together it will be OK.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||mwbackus||03-12-2000||11:36 PM||||Glad I was of some assistance, it always brightens my day when I have helped someone out.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Mike||||reg||1
